 classification MSC classification Report number arXiv identifier DOI ORCID arXiv author ID Help pages Full text\nL11: \nL12: Search\nL13: cite65†Image: arXiv logo L14: \nL15: cite6† †www.cornell.edu L16: \nL17:  open search\nL18: \nL19: GO\nL20: \nL21: open navigation menu\nL22: ## quick links\nL23: \nL24:   * cite7†Login L25:   * cite4†Help Pages†info.arxiv.org L26:   * cite8†About†info.arxiv.org L27: \nL28: # Computer Science > Cryptography and Security\nL29: \nL30: arXiv:2510.10085v1 (cs)\nL31: \nL32: [Submitted on 11 Oct 2025]\nL33: # Title:Pharmacist: Safety Alignment Data Curation for Large Language Models against Harmful Fine-tuning\nL34: \nL35: Authors:cite9†Guozhi Liu , cite10†Qi Mu , cite11†Tiansheng Huang , cite12†Xinhua Wang , cite13†Li Shen , cite14†Weiwei Lin , cite15†Zhang Li L36: \nL37: View a PDF of the paper titled Pharmacist: Safety Alignment Data Curation for Large Language Models against Harmful Fine-tuning, by Guozhi Liu and 6 other authors\nL38: \nL39: cite16†View PDF cite17†HTML (experimental) L40: > Abstract:Harmful fine-tuning issues present significant safety challenges for fine-tuning-as-a-service in large language models. Existing alignment-stage defenses, e.g., Vaccine, Repnoise, Booster, and T-Vaccine, mitigate harmful fine-tuning issues by enhancing the model's robustness during the alignment phase. While these methods have been proposed to mitigate the issue, they often overlook a critical upstream factor: the role of the original safety-alignment data.\nL41: We observe that their defense performance and computational efficiency remain constrained by the quality and composition of the alignment dataset. To address this limitation, we propose Pharmacist, a safety alignment data curation solution that enhances defense against harmful fine-tuning by selecting a high-quality and safety-critical core subset from the original alignment data. The core idea of Pharmacist is to train an alignment data selector to rank alignment data.\nL42: Specifically, up-ranking high-quality and safety-critical alignment data, down-ranking low-quality and non-safety-critical data. Empirical results indicate that models trained on datasets selected by Pharmacist outperform those trained on datasets selected by existing selection methods in both defense and inference performance. In addition, Pharmacist can be effectively integrated with mainstream alignment-stage defense methods.\nL43: For example, when applied to RepNoise and T-Vaccine, using the dataset selected by Pharmacist instead of the full dataset leads to improvements in defense performance by 2.60\\% and 3.30\\%, respectively, and enhances inference performance by 3.50\\% and 1.10\\%. Notably, it reduces training time by 56.83\\% and 57.63\\%, respectively. Our code is available at cite18†this https URL†github.com .\nL44: Subjects: Cryptography and Security (cs.CR); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\nL45: Cite as: cite19†arXiv:2510.10085 [cs.CR]\nL46:   (or  cite20†arXiv:2510.10085v1 [cs.CR] for this version)\nL47:   cite21†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2510.10085†doi.org L48: \nL49: Focus to learn more\nL50: \nL51:   arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL52: ## Submission history\nL53: \nL54: From: Guozhi Liu [cite22†view email ]\nL55: [v1] Sat, 11 Oct 2025 07:55:55 UTC (186 KB)\nL56: \nL57: Full-text links:\nL58: ## Access Paper:\nL59: \nL60:  View a PDF of the paper titled Pharmacist: Safety Alignment Data Curation for Large Language Models against Harmful Fine-tuning, by Guozhi Liu and 6 other authors\nL61: \nL62:   * cite16†View PDF L63:   * cite17†HTML (experimental) L64:   * cite23†TeX Source  L65: \nL66: cite24†view license L67: \nL68: Current browse context:\nL69: \nL70: cs.CR\nL71: \nL72: cite25†< prev |   cite26†next > L73: \nL74: cite27†new |  cite28†recent | cite29†2025-10 L75: \nL76: Change to browse by:\nL77: \nL78: cite30†cs L79: cite31†cs.AI L80: cite32†cs.LG L81: ### References & Citations\nL82: \nL83:   * cite33†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L84:   * cite34†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L85:   * cite35†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L86: \nL87: export BibTeX citation Loading...\nL88: \nL89: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL90: \nL91: ×\nL92: \nL93: loading...\nL94: \nL95: Data provided by:\nL96: \nL97: ### Bookmark\nL98: \nL99: cite36† †www.bibsonomy.org cite37† †reddit.com L100: \nL101: Bibliographic Tools\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2510.11235v1] AI Alignment Strategies from a Risk Perspective: Independent Safety Mechanisms or Shared Failures? (https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.11235v1)\nciteturn2733view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.11235v1\",\"lineno\":14}); Total lines: 160\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Artificial Intelligence\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 13 Oct 2025]\nL9: # Title:AI Alignment Strategies from a Risk Perspective: Independent Safety Mechanisms or Shared Failures?\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite5†Leonard Dung , cite6†Florian Mai L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled AI Alignment Strategies from a Risk Perspective: Independent Safety Mechanisms or Shared Failures?, by Leonard Dung and 1 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite7†View PDF cite8†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:AI alignment research aims to develop techniques to ensure that AI systems do not cause harm. However, every alignment technique has failure modes, which are conditions in which there is a non-negligible chance that the technique fails to provide safety.\nL17: As a strategy for risk mitigation, the AI safety community has increasingly adopted a defense-in-depth framework: Conceding that there is no single technique which guarantees safety, defense-in-depth consists in having multiple redundant protections against safety failure, such that safety can be maintained even if some protections fail. However, the success of defense-in-depth depends on how (un)correlated failure modes are across alignment techniques.\nL18: For example, if all techniques had the exact same failure modes, the defense-in-depth approach would provide no additional protection at all. In this paper, we analyze 7 representative alignment techniques and 7 failure modes to understand the extent to which they overlap. We then discuss our results' implications for understanding the current level of risk and how to prioritize AI alignment research in the future.\nL19: Comments:  | under review\nL20: Subjects:  | Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\nL21: Cite as:  | cite9†arXiv:2510.11235 [cs.AI]\nL22:    | (or cite10†arXiv:2510.11235v1 [cs.AI] for this version)\nL23:    | cite11†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2510.11235†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL24: ## Submission history\nL25: \nL26: From: Florian Mai [cite12†view email ]\nL27: [v1] Mon, 13 Oct 2025 10:16:59 UTC (27 KB)\nL28: \nL29: Full-text links:\nL30: \nL31: ## Access Paper:\nL32: \nL33: View a PDF of the paper titled AI Alignment Strategies from a Risk Perspective: Independent Safety Mechanisms or Shared Failures?, by Leonard Dung and 1 other authors\nL34: \nL35:   * cite7†View PDF L36:   * cite8†HTML (experimental) L37:   * cite13†TeX Source L38: \nL39: cite14†view license L40: ### Current browse context:\nL41: \nL42: cs.AI\nL43: \nL44: cite15†< prev |   cite16†next > L45: \nL46: cite17†new | cite18†recent | cite19†2025-10 L47: \nL48: Change to browse by:\nL49: \nL50: cite20†cs L51: \nL52: ### References & Citations\nL53: \nL54:   * cite21†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L55:   * cite2