te10†Teng Wang , cite11†Junjie Wu , cite12†Changwang Zhang , cite13†Jun wang L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled PoU: Proof-of-Use to Counter Tool-Call Hacking in DeepResearch Agents, by SHengjie Ma and 7 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite14†View PDF cite15†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) agents, such as recent DeepResearch-style systems, extend large language models (LLMs) with autonomous information-seeking capabilities through external tools. While reinforcement learning (RL) has enabled impressive multi-step reasoning, we identify a previously overlooked failure mode, Tool-Call Hacking, where agents inflate reward signals by issuing superficially correct tool calls without genuinely leveraging the retrieved evidence.\nL17: This results in (i) mode collapse into repetitive reliance on a single source and (ii) spurious grounding, where answers are only weakly supported by cited content.\nL18: > To address this, we propose Proof-of-Use (PoU), an evidence-grounded RL framework that enforces verifiable causal links between retrieved evidence, reasoning traces, and final answers. PoU operationalizes this through a unified step-wise contract combining syntactic citation validation, perturbation-based sensitivity rewards, and answer-evidence alignment objectives, ensuring that tool usage remains both interpretable and functionally grounded.\nL19: > Across seven QA benchmarks spanning in-domain, out-of-domain, and out-of-tool-distribution settings, PoU consistently outperforms strong DeepResearch baselines in factual accuracy, evidence faithfulness, and tool-routing balance. These findings highlight the necessity of grounding RL-trained agents not merely in task outcomes but in the causal use of retrieved information, offering a principled path toward trustworthy retrieval-augmented reasoning.\nL20: Subjects:  | Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\nL21: Cite as:  | cite16†arXiv:2510.10931 [cs.AI]\nL22:    | (or cite17†arXiv:2510.10931v1 [cs.AI] for this version)\nL23:    | cite18†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2510.10931†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL24: ## Submission history\nL25: \nL26: From: Shengjie Ma [cite19†view email ]\nL27: [v1] Mon, 13 Oct 2025 02:45:37 UTC (343 KB)\nL28: cite5†[v2] Fri, 23 Jan 2026 05:07:44 UTC (620 KB)\nL29: \nL30: Full-text links:\nL31: \nL32: ## Access Paper:\nL33: \nL34: View a PDF of the paper titled PoU: Proof-of-Use to Counter Tool-Call Hacking in DeepResearch Agents, by SHengjie Ma and 7 other authors\nL35: \nL36:   * cite14†View PDF L37:   * cite15†HTML (experimental) L38:   * cite20†TeX Source L39: \nL40: cite21†view license L41: ### Current browse context:\nL42: \nL43: cs.AI\nL44: \nL45: cite22†< prev |   cite23†next > L46: \nL47: cite24†new | cite25†recent | cite26†2025-10 L48: \nL49: Change to browse by:\nL50: \nL51: cite27†cs L52: \nL53: ### References & Citations\nL54: \nL55:   * cite28†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L56:   * cite29†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L57:   * cite30†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L58: \nL59: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL60: \nL61: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL62: \nL63: [Button: ×]\nL64: \nL65: loading...\nL66: \nL67: Data provided by:\nL68: \nL69: ### Bookmark\nL70: \nL71: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL72: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL73: \nL74: [Input] Bibliographic Explorer Toggle\nL75: \nL76: Bibliographic Explorer (cite31†What is the Explorer?†info.arxiv.org )\nL77: \nL78: [Input] Connected Papers Toggle\nL79: \nL80: Connected Papers (cite32†What is Connected Papers?†www.connectedpapers.com )\nL81: \nL82: [Input] Litmaps Toggle\nL83: \nL84: Litmaps (cite33†What is Litmaps?†www.litmaps.co )\nL85: \nL86: [Input] scite.ai Toggle\nL87: \nL88: scite Smart Citations (cite34†What are Smart Citations?†www.scite.ai )\nL89: \nL90: [Input] Code, Data, Media\nL91: # Code, Data and Media Associate